-- Question banks for the trust, partnership, sales and information-management certifications.
-- Why: the supplier-verification, trust and safety, partnerships, sales-director and doc-intel seats are
-- certified against CFE, CSAP, CSP and CIP. Each bank is 40 questions at real exam difficulty (scenario and
-- application, four options, one key, a two-sentence rationale), the syllabus area named as the issuing body
-- names it (ACFE four sections, ASAP alliance lifecycle, NASP sales process, AIIM information-management
-- domains), ~25% marked 'hard', keys spread ten per letter. Identity and trade-licence verification, collusion
-- and circumvention, supplier-document red flags and records retention are over-weighted because those are the
-- jobs these seats actually do; up to five questions per bank test the UAE variant.
-- Each bank is guarded so the first author of a shared certification wins.
-- role_certification rows and the sitter are authored by a sibling engineer; this file only writes question banks.
--
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_trust_partnerships.sql --apply --ddl

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','A buyer approves invoices from a supplier whose registered address matches an employee home address in payroll, and no goods received notes exist for any of the invoices. Which scheme does this pattern most directly describe?','Inventory larceny by a warehouse clerk','A shell company billing scheme run by an employee','Skimming of unrecorded sales','Check tampering by an authorised signatory','B','A shell company scheme uses an entity with no operations to bill for goods or services never delivered, which is why receiving evidence is absent. The address match to an employee record is the classic link between the fictitious vendor and the insider approving it.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','Customer A complains that a payment made three weeks ago is still open. The cashier posted Customer B later payment to the Customer A balance on the day the complaint arrived. What is the most likely scheme?','Register disbursement scheme','Fictitious refund scheme','Unrecorded sales skimming','Lapping of accounts receivable','D','Lapping conceals a stolen receipt by applying a later customer payment to the earlier customer balance, so the theft rolls forward rather than showing as a shortage. The tell is the timing: the covering entry appears only when the first customer complains.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','hard','A supplier invoices at prices above the signed contract while quantities and delivery dates reconcile to receiving records, and the buyer accepted paid travel from that supplier. To quantify the loss, which measurement is the correct starting point?','The difference between invoiced unit prices and contract unit prices on delivered quantities','The full value of every invoice paid to that supplier','The market value of the travel accepted by the buyer','The gross margin the supplier earned on the account','A','The economic loss in a bribery-assisted overbilling case is the price differential on goods actually received, not the whole spend, because the goods had value. The travel is evidence of the corrupt inducement and is measured separately, not as the loss itself.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','A new payroll record was created after the period cutoff with a bank account identical to the approving supervisor account and with no benefit elections or leave history. Which scheme fits best?','Falsified overtime claims','Commission scheme on inflated sales','A ghost employee scheme','Expense reimbursement multiple-reimbursement scheme','C','A ghost employee is a payroll record for a person who does not work for the organisation, so the record lacks the ordinary human traces of elections and leave. The shared bank account identifies the person who is actually collecting the pay.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','Three bidders for one tender submit quotations with identical typographical errors, sequential document numbers from the same print shop and prices that differ by an exact fixed percentage. What does this most strongly indicate?','Legitimate price benchmarking among competitors','A market with only one manufacturer','Bid rigging through complementary bidding','A procurement system data entry error','C','Complementary or cover bidding means losing bids are prepared to make a chosen winner look competitive, which is why the documents share an origin. Identical errors and a constant price step are the standard documentary fingerprints of a single preparer.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','hard','A controller records a large accrual reversal in the final days of a quarter with no supporting calculation, then reinstates it in the first week of the next quarter. Which financial statement scheme does this pattern indicate?','Improper timing of expense recognition to manage earnings','Asset misappropriation through cash larceny','Concealed related-party purchase','Payroll falsification','A','Reversing and reinstating an accrual around a period end moves expense between periods to hit a reported result, which is a timing-difference financial statement fraud. The absence of a supporting calculation shows the entry was driven by the target, not by the underlying obligation.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','hard','A trade-finance file shows the same bill of lading number supporting two separate advances from two different lenders for one shipment. Which conclusion is best supported?','A currency translation error in the loan ledger','A permissible assignment of the same collateral to two lenders','The shipping line issued duplicate originals as a routine practice','Double financing of a single trade receivable','D','Double financing pledges one underlying shipment to more than one financier so that funds exceed the real trade, and reuse of a single transport document is the mechanism. Routine duplicate originals do not create two separate advances against the same goods.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','An expense report claims a hotel stay supported by a booking confirmation rather than a paid folio, and the same dates appear on a second report submitted to a client for reimbursement. Which scheme is present?','Fictitious vendor scheme','Multiple reimbursement of the same expense','Cash register skimming','Understated liabilities','B','Multiple reimbursement means one cost is claimed more than once, here from the employer and the client. A booking confirmation is not proof of payment, which is why the paid folio is the control document.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','hard','A warehouse shows perpetual inventory matching the ledger, but physical counts are short and the shortfall is written off each quarter as shrinkage approved by one supervisor. What should the examiner suspect first?','Non-cash asset misappropriation concealed by write-offs','Revenue recognised too early','Improper capitalisation of costs','A systems rounding fault in the perpetual records','A','Concealing stolen inventory by routing the gap through an approved write-off keeps the ledger balanced while the goods leave. Concentration of approval in one person is the control weakness that makes the concealment repeatable.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Financial Transactions and Fraud Schemes','exam','A company reports rising revenue while days sales outstanding lengthens sharply and cash from operations falls. Which analytical conclusion is most appropriate?','The pattern indicates payroll fraud','The trend proves fraud has occurred','The pattern is a red flag warranting testing of revenue cutoff and collectability','The pattern is normal and needs no work','C','Analytical relationships raise inference, not proof, so the correct response is targeted testing rather than a conclusion. Revenue that does not convert to cash points to cutoff, fictitious sales or collectability, which is where the testing goes.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','An examiner wants to record a telephone call with a suspect. What is the controlling first question?','Whether the recording will be used at trial','Whether the applicable jurisdiction requires one-party or all-party consent','Whether the call is longer than five minutes','Whether the suspect is an employee','B','Interception of communications is governed by statute, and jurisdictions differ between one-party and all-party consent, so the legal test comes before any operational choice. Getting this wrong can make the recording inadmissible and expose the examiner to liability.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','During an internal interview an employee asks whether the examiner is a police officer and whether he must answer. Which response is correct?','Terminate the interview immediately in every case','State that the examiner is acting under police authority to secure cooperation','Refuse to answer and continue questioning','State truthfully that the examiner is not law enforcement and explain the nature of the interview','D','Misrepresenting authority can vitiate any admission and expose the examiner and the employer to claims. A truthful statement of role preserves the evidential value of whatever the employee then chooses to say.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','hard','Which element distinguishes fraud from an innocent misstatement in most common law formulations?','That the statement was made in writing','That the perpetrator profited personally','The size of the loss suffered by the victim','A material false statement made knowingly, with intent that the victim rely on it, and reliance causing loss','D','Fraud requires a material false statement, knowledge of its falsity, intent to induce reliance, actual reliance and resulting damage. Loss size, form and personal profit are evidentially useful but are not the defining elements.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','An examiner collects a laptop from a suspect desk and hands it to a colleague, who images it two days later. Nobody documented the interim custody. What is the primary consequence?','The image is unusable for any purpose','The chain of custody gap invites a challenge to authenticity and weight of the evidence','The evidence becomes privileged','The gap is irrelevant if a hash is produced later','B','Chain of custody proves that the item examined is the item seized and was not altered. A documented gap does not automatically exclude the evidence but hands the other side a straightforward attack on its integrity.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','Counsel instructs an examiner to conduct an investigation to advise on litigation risk. What is the effect on the resulting report?','Privilege depends only on marking the document confidential','The report is automatically public','The report may attract legal professional privilege where it was prepared for the purpose of legal advice','Privilege attaches to every internal investigation regardless of purpose','C','Privilege turns on the dominant purpose for which the work was created and the relationship with the legal adviser, not on labels. Investigations run for ordinary business purposes generally do not attract it.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','A supplier being onboarded in Dubai presents a trade licence. Which check gives the strongest assurance that the trading name and activity are genuine?','Verifying the licence number and status directly with the issuing authority, and matching the listed activity to the goods offered','Confirming the supplier has a website using the same name','Asking the supplier for a second copy of the licence','Accepting the printed licence because it carries a stamp','A','Verification means confirming with the source register, not inspecting the document the subject supplied. Matching the licensed activity to what is being sold detects entities trading outside their permitted scope.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','hard','A UAE onboarding file records the shareholders of a corporate supplier but stops at the first corporate layer. What is the deficiency?','The file should record the auditor instead','No deficiency, corporate shareholders satisfy the requirement','The ultimate beneficial owner has not been identified through the ownership chain to the natural persons who ultimately own or control','Only the trade licence expiry needs checking','C','Beneficial ownership rules require tracing ownership and control to natural persons, because layering corporate shareholders is exactly how control is hidden. Stopping at the first layer leaves the person who benefits unknown.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','A prospective counterparty name closely matches an entry on the UN Consolidated List and on the UAE Local Terrorist List. What is the correct immediate action?','Proceed while asking the counterparty to confirm it is not the listed party','Treat it as a potential match, hold onboarding, resolve the match against identifiers and report as required before any dealing','Ignore the match because the spelling differs slightly','Complete the transaction and screen again at year end','B','Sanctions and terrorist-list obligations bite before any dealing, so the relationship must be held while identifiers are used to confirm or discount the match. Self-certification by the subject is never a resolution of a screening hit.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','An examiner in a civil matter wants documents held by a third party bank. What is the appropriate route?','Use a pretext call to obtain account details','Ask the suspect to request the records on the examiner behalf','Approach a bank employee informally for copies','Use lawful process such as a subpoena or court order through counsel','D','Third party records are obtained through lawful process, which protects both admissibility and the examiner. Pretexting for financial records is unlawful in many jurisdictions and taints the resulting evidence.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Law','exam','An employee is dismissed after an investigation and the examiner repeats the allegation to an industry contact who had no need to know. What is the principal exposure?','A defamation claim, because the statement was published beyond any qualified privilege','No exposure, since the allegation was investigated','Copyright infringement','Breach of contract by the employer only','A','Qualified privilege protects communications to those with a corresponding interest or duty to receive them. Repeating an allegation outside that circle removes the protection and creates a defamation risk regardless of the investigation quality.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','An examiner plans interviews for a suspected procurement fraud involving one buyer and three colleagues. In what order should they generally be conducted?','Neutral third parties and corroborative witnesses first, the suspect last','All parties simultaneously to prevent collusion','Only the suspect, since others add nothing','The suspect first to test his account against the documents','A','Interviewing outward-in builds the documentary and testimonial base so the suspect interview can be conducted with knowledge rather than hope. Approaching the suspect first surrenders the advantage and invites destruction of evidence.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','During an admission-seeking interview the subject makes a first admission. What should the examiner do next?','Present the entire document file for comment','Advise the subject that dismissal is certain','Immediately end the interview to preserve the admission','Obtain a full description of the scheme including scope, method, others involved and disposition of the proceeds','D','A bare admission is of limited value without scope, method, participants and the fate of the proceeds, which are the elements that support recovery and proof. The examiner develops these while the subject is still in an admitting frame.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','hard','A fraud examiner must analyse two years of disbursement data to find duplicate and fictitious payments. Which test is most likely to surface a shell vendor?','Sorting payments by descending amount','Matching vendor bank accounts, addresses and phone numbers against employee master data','Recalculating the general ledger trial balance','Confirming the aggregate spend with the finance director','B','Shell vendors controlled by insiders are exposed by identity overlaps between vendor and employee master data, because the money must reach a real person. Large-value sorting misses the many-small-invoice pattern that shell schemes usually adopt.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','Which describes the correct handling of an original questioned document received during an examination?','Return it to the suspect after copying','Annotate it with the examiner findings in pen','Preserve it unaltered, work from copies, and record who held it and when','Store it with the working papers of unrelated matters','C','Originals carry evidence in ink, paper, indentations and alterations that annotation destroys. Working from copies with a documented custody record preserves both the evidence and its authenticity.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','A supplier submits a bank reference letter as part of onboarding. Which feature is the strongest red flag of a fabricated document?','The letter is dated in the current month','The letter names the supplier trading name','The letter is on bank letterhead','The letter uses a generic free email domain for the signing officer and the branch address does not exist in the bank published branch list','D','Fabricated references usually copy the visual form but fail on verifiable externals such as the branch and the officer contact channel. Verification against the bank published data, not the appearance of the document, is the control.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','An examiner reconstructs a suspect assets, liabilities and living expenses to show unexplained wealth. Which method is being used?','The net worth method of proving income indirectly','A vertical common-size analysis','A cutoff test','A systems walkthrough','A','The net worth method infers unreported income from increases in assets and expenditures over known sources. It is used when direct evidence of the receipts is unavailable.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','hard','Two apparently unrelated bidders share a registered agent, a bank account signatory and an accountant, and always bid on the same tenders. Which analytic approach best evidences the relationship?','Interviewing the tender committee first','A word-frequency analysis of the tender text','Link analysis mapping shared identifiers, directors, addresses and accounts across entities','Sampling ten invoices from each entity','C','Link analysis makes hidden common control visible by mapping shared attributes across entities. It converts scattered identifiers into a demonstrable relationship that supports a collusion finding.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','A written examination report includes the examiner opinion that the suspect is guilty of fraud. Why is this improper?','Reports must never mention the suspect','Guilt is a legal conclusion for the trier of fact, and the examiner reports findings and evidence','Opinions are prohibited in all professional reports','The report would then require notarisation','B','The examiner presents evidence and findings; guilt is determined by the court or tribunal. Stating a conclusion of guilt exceeds the professional role and undermines the credibility of the report.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','An examiner needs to interview a witness who is nervous and peripheral to the scheme. Which question type should open the interview?','A leading question naming the suspect','An open question inviting a narrative account of the process','An accusatory question','A closed question about a specific invoice number','B','Open questions produce narrative from which detail and inconsistencies can be developed, and they settle a nervous witness. Narrowing to specifics comes after the account is on the record.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Investigation','exam','Digital evidence must be collected from a running server that cannot be shut down. What is the preferred approach?','Ask the administrator to email the files','Copy the relevant files with the operating system file manager','Perform a documented live acquisition preserving volatile data with hashing and a contemporaneous log','Wait until the next maintenance window and image then','C','A live acquisition captures volatile data that a later image would lose and records what was done, by whom and when. Hashing at collection is what allows the image to be shown later as unaltered.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','Which change most directly attacks the opportunity element of the fraud triangle in a purchasing function?','Segregating vendor master maintenance from invoice approval and payment release','Increasing bonus targets','Running an annual staff survey','Publishing a values statement','A','Opportunity is created by concentration of incompatible duties, and separating vendor creation from approval and payment removes the ability to invent and pay a vendor alone. Culture initiatives address rationalisation, not opportunity.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','ACFE occupational fraud research consistently finds which detection method is the most common?','Accidental discovery','Regulatory notification','External audit','Tips','D','Tips are consistently the largest single detection source, and organisations with reporting hotlines detect sooner and lose less. This is why a promoted, protected reporting channel is a primary control rather than an optional extra.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','hard','A company wants to reduce fraud losses at lowest cost. Which combination is best supported by the evidence?','A larger internal audit team alone','An anonymous reporting mechanism actively promoted, plus targeted anti-fraud training and management review','Doubling external audit fees','Requiring two signatures on all cheques only','B','Reporting channels and awareness training shorten the duration of schemes, which is the main driver of loss size. Adding audit resource alone strengthens a detection route that finds a much smaller share of cases.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','A code of conduct requires annual conflict of interest declarations. A buyer declares nothing while his spouse is a director of a bidding supplier. Which control would most likely have detected this?','Storing declarations in the personnel file','Requiring the code to be signed twice a year','Matching declared and undeclared relationships against corporate registry director data for bidders','Increasing the purchase order approval limit','C','A declaration is only as good as its verification, and registry data provides an independent test of the relationship. Without a matching step the control depends entirely on the honesty of the person with the conflict.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','Which indicator is among the strongest behavioural red flags identified in occupational fraud studies?','Living beyond apparent means combined with unwillingness to share duties','Frequent use of annual leave','Requesting additional training','Long tenure in the organisation','A','Living beyond means and refusal to let anyone near the work are the two behavioural flags most frequently observed before detection. Refusal to share duties preserves the concealment the scheme depends on.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','A fraud risk assessment should be primarily organised around what?','The audit fee budget','The list of prior year audit findings','The organisation chart','Specific fraud schemes that could occur, their likelihood, significance and the controls that address them','D','A fraud risk assessment is scheme-based: it identifies what could be done, by whom and how it would be concealed, then tests whether controls address it. Organising by department or prior findings misses schemes nobody has yet attempted.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','hard','A UAE supplier onboarding process keeps copies of passports, Emirates ID and establishment cards. Under the UAE personal data protection framework, which practice is correct?','Publish the documents on an internal shared drive for transparency','Delete all records immediately after onboarding completes','Retain all copies indefinitely to be safe','Retain personal data only as long as necessary for the purpose, with a defined retention period and secure disposal','D','Data protection principles require purpose limitation and storage limitation, so records are kept for a defined necessary period and then securely disposed of. Indefinite retention and open internal publication both create exposure without serving the verification purpose.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','Which management action most damages a fraud prevention programme?','Quietly allowing a senior employee to resign after a proven fraud with no consequence communicated','Publishing anonymised outcomes of disciplinary cases','Rotating duties in high risk roles','Investigating a small theft thoroughly','A','Perceived detection and consequence are what deter, and a silent exit for a senior offender signals the opposite to everyone who notices. Inconsistent consequence also feeds the rationalisation that everyone does it.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','A vendor requests a change of bank account by email from a domain differing by one character from the known domain. What is the correct control response?','Delay the payment but update the account now','Update the account and confirm by reply email','Verify the change by calling a previously known telephone number for the vendor and require documented dual approval before the master data change','Update the account because the invoice details match','C','Payment diversion works by capturing the change of bank details through a channel the fraudster controls, so verification must use a previously known independent channel. Dual approval on vendor master changes prevents a single compromised inbox from moving the money.'),
('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)','Fraud Prevention and Deterrence','exam','Which statement about anti-fraud controls is most accurate?','Preventive controls make detective controls unnecessary','Detective controls also deter, because the perceived likelihood of detection reduces the willingness to offend','Deterrence has no relationship to detection','Only preventive controls are cost effective','B','Deterrence operates through perceived risk of being caught, so visible detective controls change behaviour as well as finding schemes. Prevention and detection are complementary because no preventive control set is complete.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','A company can obtain a capability by building it, buying the owner or allying with a specialist. Which condition most favours an alliance?','The capability is needed quickly, is outside the core, and the market is uncertain enough that ownership would lock in risk','The target company is available at a low valuation','The company has surplus cash and no acquisition pipeline','The capability is core to the company competitive advantage and is fully understood internally','A','Alliances suit situations where speed matters, the capability is non-core and uncertainty makes irreversible ownership expensive. Capabilities central to advantage are normally built or bought so control is retained.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','Which statement best describes strategic fit in partner selection?','The partner is not currently working with a competitor','The partners are of similar size','The partner objectives, market focus and capabilities are complementary and both sides gain from the same outcome','The partners use the same technology stack','C','Strategic fit means the alliance advances both sets of objectives through complementary contributions, which is what sustains commitment. Size, tooling and exclusivity are attributes to assess but none of them creates mutual gain.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','hard','A partner passes commercial due diligence but the alliance manager notes the partner rewards its sales team only on direct product revenue. What risk does this create?','No risk, since compensation is internal to the partner','A structural misalignment that will starve the alliance of partner selling effort regardless of the signed commitments','A tax exposure for the alliance','A currency risk on joint invoices','B','Behaviour follows compensation, so an incentive plan that pays nothing for alliance revenue predicts weak execution whatever the agreement says. Cultural and incentive fit is assessed alongside strategic and operational fit for exactly this reason.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','Before approaching a prospective partner, an alliance team builds a business case. Which element is most often missing and most damaging when it is?','A list of prior alliances in the industry','The travel budget for partner meetings','The partner logo and branding assets','A defined value hypothesis with the metrics that will show whether the alliance is working','D','Without an agreed value hypothesis and measures, an alliance cannot be steered or judged and drifts into activity without outcome. Metrics defined before signature also shape what the agreement must require of each side.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','A prospective reseller partner in the UAE offers to represent the company in Dubai. Which verification is the minimum before signing?','A reference from another supplier','Proof that the partner has an office lease','A signed letter of intent from the partner sales director','Confirmation of the trade licence number, validity and licensed activity with the issuing authority, plus establishment card and authorised signatory evidence','D','A commercial agreement binds the licensed legal entity, so licence status, permitted activity and signing authority must be verified with the register before signature. References and premises say nothing about whether the entity may lawfully perform the activity or whether the signatory can bind it.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','Which portfolio-level question should an alliance function answer before adding a new partner in an existing segment?','Whether the new partner will accept the standard agreement','Whether the new partner overlaps existing partners in a way that creates channel conflict without adding coverage','Whether the new partner attends the same conferences','Whether the new partner has a larger marketing budget','B','Portfolio management is about coverage and conflict, not the count of partners, and an overlapping partner can reduce total effort by making every deal contested. Adding partners without a coverage gap is a common cause of portfolio decay.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','hard','Two partners each claim credit for the same customer opportunity. Which upstream design decision would have prevented the dispute?','A quarterly partner satisfaction survey','A higher discount for the first partner to register','Defined rules of engagement with deal registration, territory or segment definition and a documented conflict resolution path','A joint press release at launch','C','Deal registration and rules of engagement decide ownership before the money is on the table, which is the only time the parties can agree calmly. Discounts and communications do not resolve who owns the opportunity.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Strategy and Analysis','exam','What distinguishes an alliance from a simple vendor contract?','The parties share risk, invest jointly and pursue an outcome neither can achieve alone, under shared governance','The alliance never involves payment','The alliance requires an equity stake','The alliance is always longer in duration','A','An alliance is defined by shared investment, shared risk and joint governance towards a common outcome, not by duration or equity. A vendor contract exchanges a defined deliverable for payment with no shared outcome.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','During negotiation the partner asks for exclusivity in a region. What is the most balanced structure?','Grant exclusivity but keep it undocumented','Grant perpetual exclusivity to secure commitment','Grant exclusivity conditional on defined performance thresholds with a defined review and a conversion to non-exclusive if the thresholds are missed','Refuse exclusivity in all circumstances','C','Performance-conditioned exclusivity buys commitment while protecting coverage if the partner does not deliver. Unconditional exclusivity transfers a market to a partner with no obligation to serve it.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','Which provision most directly addresses the risk that a partner approaches the customers it met through the alliance and contracts with them directly?','A confidentiality clause','A non-circumvention clause with a defined protected customer list, duration and remedy','A force majeure clause','A most favoured nation clause','B','Non-circumvention prevents a party from bypassing the counterparty to deal directly with relationships obtained through the alliance. It only works if the protected parties, the period and the remedy are specified rather than described generally.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','hard','An alliance will create jointly developed software. Which intellectual property structure most often avoids later deadlock?','The larger party owns everything created','Ownership is left to be agreed at the end of the project','Joint ownership of all foreground intellectual property with no use rules','Each party retains its background intellectual property, foreground ownership is allocated by defined categories, and each party receives the licence it needs for its own field of use','D','Deadlock arises when jointly owned property requires mutual consent for every use, so ownership is allocated and cross-licences give each party the rights it actually needs. Leaving allocation until the end negotiates it at the point of maximum conflict.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','Which negotiating behaviour best supports a durable alliance?','Trading terms against a shared understanding of each side interests, so the resulting economics remain viable for both','Deferring all difficult terms to a side letter','Insisting on the standard template without discussion','Extracting the maximum concession on every term while leverage exists','A','An alliance is executed by the counterparty for years, so terms that leave the partner economics unviable produce non-performance rather than advantage. Interest-based trading finds the terms each side actually values.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','What is the purpose of defining an alliance governance structure in the agreement?','To specify decision rights, escalation paths and the meeting cadence that will resolve issues before they become disputes','To limit the number of people who may contact the partner','To make the agreement longer','To satisfy the legal department','A','Governance decides who decides, and an escalation path converts disagreements into decisions instead of stalemates. Alliances fail more often on unresolved operational issues than on the commercial terms.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','A partner insists that the alliance agreement omit termination provisions to signal commitment. How should the alliance manager respond?','Sign and add termination terms later by email','Reduce the agreement to a memorandum of understanding','Agree, since termination clauses damage trust','Explain that defined exit terms including notice, transition of customers, data return and surviving obligations protect both parties and make commitment safer','D','Exit provisions are what allow both parties to invest, because each knows how value and customers are handled if the alliance ends. An alliance without exit terms is not more committed, only more exposed.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','hard','An alliance will exchange customer contact data across borders. Which term is essential in the agreement?','A clause stating that both parties will act in good faith','Data protection terms setting the lawful basis, permitted purposes, security obligations, cross-border transfer conditions and deletion on termination','A clause naming a data centre vendor','A clause requiring quarterly marketing reviews','B','Sharing personal data creates obligations that must be allocated in the contract, including purpose limitation, security, transfer conditions and deletion. Good faith language does not establish a lawful basis or bind the partner to any handling standard.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Design and Negotiation','exam','How should the joint value proposition be documented at the design stage?','As an appendix to the pricing schedule only','As a slogan for the launch campaign','As a specific statement of the customer problem, the combined offering, the target segment and the evidence of benefit, agreed by both parties','As an internal note for the sales team','C','A joint value proposition is the operating instruction for both sales forces, so it must name the problem, the combined offer, the segment and the proof. A slogan cannot be sold against and does not align the two organisations.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','What is the most common cause of an alliance losing momentum in the first ninety days?','The launch event was too small','Too many joint customer meetings were scheduled','The partner logo was not approved','No joint operating plan with named owners, dates and first deliverables, so activity depends on goodwill','D','Momentum comes from a plan with owners and dates that converts the agreement into work. Without it the alliance depends on individuals finding time, which is the classic slow failure.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','A new alliance is launched. Which enablement step most improves early results?','Training both sales teams on the joint value proposition, the rules of engagement and how to register and route a deal','Issuing a press release','Adding the partner logo to the website','Sending the partner a copy of the signed agreement','A','Sellers act on what they can explain and on a process they know how to follow, so joint enablement is the step that produces pipeline. Announcements build awareness but do not change selling behaviour.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','hard','Six weeks after launch the alliance has meetings but no pipeline. Which diagnostic sequence is most appropriate?','Increase the marketing budget','Escalate immediately to both executive sponsors','Test whether the joint value proposition is understood, whether sellers are compensated for alliance deals and whether the deal routing process works, then escalate what is structural','Replace the partner','C','Diagnosing the value proposition, incentives and process separates a communications problem from a structural one and shows the sponsors what to decide. Escalating without a diagnosis spends sponsor attention without direction.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','Which role does the alliance manager play when a partner escalates a delivery failure by the manager own company?','Defend the company position to the partner','Act as the single point of accountability, own the issue internally, keep the partner informed and hold the internal team to a resolution date','Refer the partner to the general support queue','Ask the partner to submit the complaint in writing before acting','B','The alliance manager exists to make the relationship work across the boundary, which means owning the issue inside the company rather than relaying it. Handing the partner to a general queue signals that the alliance carries no weight.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','Which measure best shows whether an alliance is producing value in its first year?','The number of joint meetings held','Sourced and influenced pipeline, joint wins and the revenue attributable to the alliance against the value hypothesis','The number of partner staff trained','The number of press mentions','B','Value is measured against the hypothesis that justified the alliance, which is normally pipeline and revenue outcomes. Activity counts are inputs and can rise while outcomes stay at zero.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','A partner sales representative registers a deal for a customer already in the company direct pipeline. What should happen?','The alliance manager decides case by case with no record','The registration is rejected without explanation','The documented rules of engagement decide the outcome, the decision is communicated with its reason, and the exception is logged','The larger deal owner wins by default','C','Applying published rules consistently and recording the outcome is what keeps partners willing to register deals at all. Unexplained or ad hoc decisions destroy the registration data the channel depends on.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','hard','An alliance depends on a partner integration that the partner engineering team keeps deprioritising. Which action best fits alliance practice?','Quantify the revenue at risk and the customer commitments affected, then take the case with the partner alliance manager to the governance body that holds the decision right','Rebuild the integration unilaterally and invoice the partner','Terminate the alliance','Publicly criticise the partner in the joint steering meeting','A','Governance exists to resolve exactly this kind of resource conflict, and it moves when the cost of inaction is quantified. Unilateral rebuilds and public criticism damage the relationship without obtaining the engineering commitment.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Launch and Implementation','exam','Which practice most improves trust between two alliance teams in the first year?','Routing all communication through the legal teams','Limiting contact to the two alliance managers','Meeting only when there is a deal to discuss','A regular operating rhythm where commitments made are tracked and closed, and bad news is raised early','D','Trust is built by kept commitments and early disclosure, both of which need a working cadence to become visible. Restricting contact starves the alliance of the working relationships that execute it.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','What should an alliance scorecard contain beyond financial results?','Only the revenue figure, to keep it simple','Leading indicators such as joint pipeline, enablement coverage and issue resolution time, alongside relationship health and the financial outcome','The partner internal profitability','The number of emails exchanged','B','Financial results are lagging, so a scorecard needs leading indicators that can still be acted upon and a relationship health measure. Balanced measurement is what allows correction before the revenue number confirms the failure.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','An executive sponsor asks what she should personally do for the alliance. What is the best answer?','Manage the partner relationship day to day','Attend the operational meetings each week','Set direction, remove internal blockers, hold the internal organisation to its alliance commitments and meet her counterpart on a defined cadence','Approve every partner discount','C','Sponsors add value by unblocking and by holding their own organisation accountable, which the alliance manager cannot do. Drawing sponsors into operational detail wastes the authority that makes them useful.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','hard','Alliance revenue is growing but the partner satisfaction survey and issue backlog are both deteriorating. What is the correct reading?','Revenue is a lagging indicator and the relationship measures are warning that the current results are being produced at an unsustainable cost to the partner','The alliance should be expanded immediately','The issue backlog is the partner responsibility','The survey is unreliable because revenue is up','A','Relationship health leads revenue, so deteriorating satisfaction with a rising backlog predicts the revenue turn rather than contradicting it. Expanding on top of an unresolved backlog accelerates the failure.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','A quarterly business review with a partner should primarily be used for what?','Renegotiating the commercial terms','Socialising with the partner executives','Presenting the company product roadmap','Reviewing performance against the joint plan, deciding on the gaps and agreeing the next quarter commitments with owners','D','A business review is a decision meeting: it compares results to plan and sets the next commitments. Roadmaps and relationships are inputs, not the purpose.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','Which situation most clearly requires escalation under an alliance governance model?','A partner request for additional marketing collateral','A disagreement about meeting scheduling','A single customer complaint about delivery timing','A repeated failure by one party to meet a committed obligation that the operational teams have been unable to resolve','D','Escalation is for issues the operating level has tried and failed to resolve, particularly repeated breach of commitments. Escalating routine requests trains the governance body to ignore escalations.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','How should an alliance manager handle a partner request for information that is competitively sensitive?','Apply the agreed information sharing boundaries, share what the alliance needs under the confidentiality terms and decline the rest with an explanation','Share nothing to be safe','Refer the request to the sales team','Share it if the partner signs a non-disclosure agreement','A','Alliances need real information flow within defined boundaries, so the answer is calibrated sharing rather than either extreme. A confidentiality agreement governs handling but does not by itself make sensitive information appropriate to share.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','hard','A partner has begun selling a competing product alongside the alliance offering, which the agreement permits. What is the most effective response?','Reduce support to the partner','Terminate for breach','Accept the position and compete for partner mindshare by improving partner economics, enablement and deal support, while measuring share of partner business','Demand exclusivity retroactively','C','Where the conduct is permitted, the lever is preference rather than enforcement, and preference is won on economics and ease of selling. Reducing support hands the segment to the competing product.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Management and Governance','exam','Which is the strongest sign that alliance governance is working?','Meetings always finish early','Issues raised at the operating level are being closed with documented decisions, and only genuinely structural matters reach the sponsors','No issues are ever raised','All decisions are made by the sponsors','B','Healthy governance resolves most matters at the lowest competent level and reserves sponsors for structural decisions. An absence of raised issues usually means they are being suppressed rather than solved.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','An alliance is meeting its targets but the market has shifted so that the joint offering addresses a shrinking segment. What is the appropriate step?','Continue unchanged until the targets are missed','Take the alliance through a transformation review that revisits the value hypothesis, scope and target segment with the partner','Terminate the alliance immediately','Reduce the alliance to a referral relationship without discussion','B','Alliances are reviewed against their strategic premise rather than only their current numbers, because a shifting market invalidates the premise before the targets fall. Transformation with the partner preserves the invested relationship while changing what it does.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','Which action should be taken first when a decision has been made to wind down an alliance?','Stop all partner support immediately','Delete the shared systems access','Announce the decision publicly','Plan the customer transition, data return and communications with the partner before any external announcement','D','Customers in flight are the first exposure in any wind-down, so their transition and the handling of shared data are planned with the partner before anything is said externally. Abrupt withdrawal creates customer harm and legal exposure.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','hard','On termination, both parties hold personal data of jointly served customers. What is the correct treatment?','Return or delete personal data according to the agreed terms and applicable law, retaining only what a legal obligation requires, with the deletion evidenced','Transfer all data to the larger party','Retain the data for future marketing since consent was given at the start','Each party may retain everything it collected','A','Termination ends the purpose for which the data was shared, so it is returned or deleted except where a legal retention obligation applies, and the deletion is evidenced. Consent given for the alliance purpose does not extend to later independent marketing.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','What is the value of a formal alliance post mortem?','It is required before any new alliance may be signed','It assigns blame for the outcome','It captures what the partner selection, design and management stages got right and wrong so the portfolio improves','It satisfies the audit requirement','C','The point of a post mortem is transferable learning about selection, design and management practice. Blame allocation stops people contributing the information that makes the review useful.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','Which contractual provisions typically survive termination of an alliance agreement?','Only the exclusivity terms','None, since the agreement has ended','Confidentiality, non-circumvention for the agreed period, intellectual property allocation, data handling and dispute resolution','Only the payment terms','C','Survival clauses keep the protections that matter after the commercial relationship stops, notably confidentiality, circumvention, intellectual property and dispute resolution. Commercial terms such as pricing naturally fall away.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','A partner proposes expanding a successful alliance into a new region. What should be tested before agreeing?','Whether the partner has the licensed entity, capability and coverage in that region and whether the value hypothesis holds in that market','Whether the expansion can be announced at the next conference','Whether the current contract mentions the region','Whether the partner wants it strongly enough','A','Expansion is a new alliance decision in a new market and is tested on capability, lawful presence and market fit rather than on enthusiasm or contractual silence. A partner strong in one market is frequently absent in the next.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','hard','During wind-down the partner asks to keep using the joint brand for three months to avoid confusing its customers. What is the best response?','Ignore the request','Allow continued use indefinitely for goodwill','Refuse and demand immediate removal in all materials','Agree a defined, documented transition licence limited in scope, channel and duration with removal obligations and evidence of completion','D','A short, bounded transition licence protects customers while keeping the brand under control, and the boundaries must be written down. Both immediate removal and open-ended use create avoidable damage.'),
('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)','Alliance Transformation and Ending','exam','Which metric best indicates that an alliance should be renewed rather than ended?','The relationship is friendly at executive level','The alliance continues to produce outcomes that neither party could achieve alone, at a cost both consider justified','The agreement has an automatic renewal clause','The partner has requested renewal','B','Renewal is justified when the alliance still creates value that is unavailable separately and both sides still find the cost worthwhile. Friendliness and contractual default are not evidence of continued value.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','A seller has one hundred inbound enquiries and capacity to work twenty. Which qualification approach is most defensible?','Work them in the order received','Work the twenty largest by stated budget','Score against defined fit and readiness criteria including need, authority, timing and ability to buy, then work the highest scoring','Work the twenty that replied fastest','C','Qualification allocates scarce selling time against the probability and value of a close, which needs fit and readiness criteria rather than a single attribute. Stated budget alone routinely selects deals with no authority or timing.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','A prospect says he is interested but that his colleague signs the contracts. What is the correct next step?','Send the proposal to the prospect and wait','Confirm the decision process and the signing authority, and agree with the prospect how and when the signatory will be involved','Send the proposal directly to the signatory without telling the prospect','Discount to accelerate the decision','B','Deals stall when the seller works with someone who cannot buy, so the process and the authority are established and a route to the signatory is agreed with the contact. Bypassing the contact usually loses the internal advocate.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','hard','A seller consistently generates meetings but few progress past the first call. Which diagnosis is most likely and what does it imply?','The territory is too small','The product needs new features','The pricing is too high, so discounting is needed','The prospects are being booked on curiosity rather than a qualified problem, so the pre-meeting qualification criteria and the opening need to change','D','A high meeting rate with low progression indicates that meetings are being booked without a qualified problem to solve. The correction is upstream in qualification and in the opening conversation, not in price.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','Which outreach approach is most consistent with professional selling practice?','A message referencing a specific, verifiable situation in the prospect business and proposing a defined next step','A message stating that the product is the best in the market','Repeated identical follow-ups until a reply arrives','A generic message sent to the largest possible list','A','Relevance to an observable situation earns the response, and a defined next step makes it easy to say yes. Generic claims and repetition consume the list without producing qualified conversations.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','A prospect requests a proposal on the first call before any discovery. What is the professional response?','Explain that a useful proposal requires understanding of requirements and constraints, and agree a short discovery step before proposing','Refuse to proposal until a purchase order is issued','Send a price list instead','Send a standard proposal immediately to show responsiveness','A','A proposal written without requirements is a price quotation and invites comparison on price alone. Agreeing a discovery step preserves the ability to propose against the actual problem.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','How should a seller treat a lead that fits the ideal customer profile but has no current need?','Continue calling weekly until interest appears','Pass it to a competitor','Disqualify permanently','Place it in a nurture cadence with a defined trigger to revisit, and continue to work active opportunities','D','Fit without timing is a future opportunity, so it is nurtured against a trigger rather than discarded or pursued at cost. Weekly pressure on a prospect with no need damages the relationship the trigger will depend on.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','hard','A seller pipeline shows coverage of three times quota but a historic win rate of ten percent on similar deals. What is the correct conclusion?','The pipeline is sufficient','Coverage is inadequate against the observed win rate and the seller must either add qualified pipeline or improve conversion, so the shortfall is quantified now','The win rate will improve because coverage is high','The quota should be reduced','B','Coverage is only meaningful against the conversion rate that actually applies, and three times coverage at ten percent conversion cannot deliver the number. Naming the gap early leaves time to act on it.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Prospecting and Qualification','exam','A prospective buyer asks the seller to route the purchase through a company owned by the buyer relative, with a markup, to avoid internal approval. What should the seller do?','Ask for the request in writing and then proceed','Agree, since the customer chooses its own suppliers','Decline, record the request and escalate internally under the ethics and anti-bribery policy','Agree if the markup is small','C','A request structured to defeat the customer own controls and enrich a related party is a corruption red flag that the seller must not facilitate. Declining and escalating protects the seller, the employer and the customer.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','Which question type most reliably surfaces the business impact of a problem in discovery?','A question about the budget holder name','A question about the preferred delivery date','A closed question confirming a feature is needed','A question exploring the consequence of the current situation in cost, time, risk or lost revenue','D','Impact questions convert a described inconvenience into a quantified cost, which is what justifies action and budget. Feature and logistics questions gather facts but do not build the case for change.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','A buyer describes a requirement in the vocabulary of a competitor product. What should the seller do?','Explore the underlying outcome the buyer needs, since the stated requirement may encode a competitor design rather than the buyer need','Tell the buyer the requirement is wrong','Withdraw from the opportunity','Match the competitor terminology and quote against it','A','A requirement written in a competitor language usually reflects that competitor architecture rather than the business outcome. Reframing to the outcome is what allows an alternative solution to be evaluated at all.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','hard','In a complex sale the economic buyer, the technical evaluator and the end user each describe a different primary problem. How should the seller proceed?','Address the end user problem because users drive adoption','Address only the economic buyer problem','Map each stakeholder problem and success criteria, find the connection between them and build a solution narrative that serves each role in its own terms','Ask them to agree a single requirement before proceeding','C','Complex sales are decided by several parties with different measures, so the seller must serve each role rather than choose one. Insisting the buying group resolve its own differences first usually stalls the deal.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','What is the main risk of a seller talking more than the buyer during discovery?','The call runs over time','The seller gathers no information and pitches against assumed needs, which produces objections later','The buyer will consider the seller unprofessional','The notes will be incomplete','B','Discovery exists to gather the information the proposal depends on, and a seller who talks fills the call with assumptions. Those assumptions surface later as objections that could have been avoided.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','How should a seller confirm understanding at the end of a discovery conversation?','Send a proposal the same day','Summarise the problem, impact, decision process and criteria back to the buyer and ask for correction, then confirm in writing','Ask the buyer to sign a requirements document','Move directly to a product demonstration','B','A summary tested against the buyer corrects misunderstanding while it is cheap and creates the shared record the proposal is built on. Proposing or demonstrating before confirmation risks solving the wrong problem convincingly.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','A buyer states a need the seller product cannot meet. What is the professional response?','Offer a discount to compensate','Imply the capability exists and manage it later','State plainly what the product does and does not do, and explore whether the need is essential or can be met another way','End the conversation','C','Misrepresenting capability produces cancellation, refund and reputational cost far beyond the deal. Testing whether the requirement is essential often reveals an acceptable alternative, and if it does not, the disqualification is fast and honest.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','hard','Which practice best distinguishes a consultative seller from a product seller in a first meeting?','Establishing the buyer current situation and desired outcome before any product content, and connecting product only to the outcomes stated','Offering a trial immediately','Quoting the reference customer list','Presenting the full product capability set early','A','Consultative selling earns the right to present by first establishing the situation and the desired outcome. Product content delivered before that is a pitch, and the buyer has no way to judge its relevance.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Needs Discovery','exam','A buyer refuses to share budget information. What is the most useful alternative line of enquiry?','Estimate the budget from company size and proceed','Reduce the price preemptively','Insist on a number before continuing','Explore what the problem currently costs, how similar investments have been approved previously and what the approval process requires','D','Understanding the cost of the problem and the approval path gives the seller what budget disclosure would have provided. Guessing or discounting without that information gives away margin blindly.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','Which structure makes a proposal most persuasive to an economic buyer?','A detailed feature list followed by price','The buyer stated problem and its quantified impact, the proposed outcome, the evidence, the investment and the next step','A company history followed by a product overview','A price comparison against competitors','B','Economic buyers approve business cases, so the proposal leads with the problem, its cost and the outcome purchased. Feature lists ask the buyer to translate capability into value, which most will not do.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','How should a seller present a price that is higher than a competitor quotation?','Explain that the company never discounts','Match the competitor price immediately','Establish the total cost and risk of each option over the period that matters to the buyer, so the comparison is on outcome rather than headline price','Avoid discussing the competitor','C','A higher price is defended by widening the frame to total cost, risk and outcome across the ownership period. Refusing to engage with the comparison leaves the buyer with only the headline numbers.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','hard','A proposal must serve both a procurement team scoring on compliance and a business sponsor buying an outcome. What is the best approach?','Structure the proposal so the compliance requirements are answered completely and the business case is stated clearly for the sponsor, without either burying the other','Send two contradictory documents','Provide only the business case and let procurement request the rest','Write one document optimised for procurement scoring','A','Both audiences must be satisfied because either can stop the deal, and a document can carry a clear business case alongside a complete compliance response. Optimising for one audience alone is the usual cause of a technically compliant loss or a scored-out favourite.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','What makes a customer reference most persuasive?','It is recent','It is provided by the vendor marketing team','It is from the largest company available','It is from a comparable situation with a stated measurable result the buyer recognises as their own problem','D','References persuade through similarity of situation and a measurable outcome the buyer can map onto their own case. Size and recency matter only where they affect comparability.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','A seller is asked to run a product demonstration for a buying committee. What preparation matters most?','Preparing a longer demonstration to cover all possibilities','Preparing the pricing slide first','Rehearsing every product feature','Selecting the sequence that shows the buyer own scenarios and success criteria, with the roles present and their measures identified','D','A demonstration persuades when the buyer sees their own scenario succeed, which requires knowing the scenarios and who judges them. Coverage of every feature dilutes attention and lengthens the meeting without adding conviction.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','How should a seller quantify value when the buyer will not supply figures?','Build the model with the buyer using ranges and the buyer own assumptions, and let the buyer own the numbers','Omit quantification entirely','Use the largest published case study result','Use vendor marketing claims as the estimate','A','A value case survives internal review only if the buyer owns the assumptions, so building it jointly with ranges beats importing a vendor figure. Numbers the buyer did not create will be discarded at the first challenge.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','hard','A buyer says the proposal is approved subject to legal review, and legal returns a redline shifting unlimited liability to the seller. What is the professional response?','Sign and disclose the change internally afterwards','Accept the redline to protect the deal','Engage the seller own legal function, explain the commercial rationale for the standard position and negotiate a bounded limitation with the buyer legal team','Reject the deal','C','Liability terms are a company risk decision, not a seller concession, and they are negotiated with the seller own legal function engaged. Accepting unlimited liability to save a deal transfers a company-scale risk for a single sale.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Presenting Value and Proposal','exam','What is the primary purpose of a mutual action plan attached to a proposal?','To demonstrate the seller process maturity','To set out the steps, owners and dates that both parties will complete to reach a decision and a successful start','To replace the contract','To record the discount approvals','B','A mutual action plan converts an intention to buy into a scheduled path with shared ownership, which is what exposes stalls early. It is not a contractual document and does not replace one.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','A buyer says the price is too high. What should the seller do first?','Offer a discount','Clarify what the objection means, whether it is a budget constraint, a value comparison or a negotiation position, before responding','Restate the value proposition','Escalate to a manager','B','Price objections carry at least three different meanings and each has a different answer, so the seller diagnoses before responding. Discounting on an unexamined objection concedes margin that was often not required.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','Which principle should govern any concession made in a sales negotiation?','Concede the smallest item first regardless of value','Never concede anything','Concede early to build goodwill','Every concession is traded for something of value and the trade is documented','D','Unilateral concessions teach the buyer that waiting produces more, so each concession is traded and recorded. Refusing all movement is equally unworkable in a negotiation with any complexity.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','hard','Late in a negotiation the buyer demands a further ten percent reduction and says a competitor has offered it. What is the strongest response?','Test the comparison for equivalence of scope and terms, restate the value at stake, and if movement is necessary trade it against scope, term length or payment terms','Withdraw from the negotiation','Offer fifteen percent to end the discussion','Match the reduction to close','A','A competitor comparison is only meaningful if the scope is equivalent, and any movement is exchanged for something that restores the economics. Matching an unverified number invites a further request.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','A buyer objects that the implementation risk is too high. Which response addresses the objection properly?','Change the subject to product capability','Assert that implementations always succeed','Acknowledge the risk, present the specific mitigations, the evidence from comparable implementations and the commitments the seller will make','Offer a discount to compensate for the risk','C','A risk objection is answered with mitigations and evidence, not with reassurance or price. Discounting a risk objection leaves the risk in place and reduces the resources available to manage it.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','What should a seller do when an objection is raised that the seller cannot answer?','Refer the buyer to the website','Guess at an answer to maintain momentum','Acknowledge it, commit to a specific time to respond with the correct answer, and deliver on that commitment','Deflect until the buyer forgets','C','Credibility survives an unknown answer but not a wrong one, and a kept commitment to respond builds trust. Deflection converts a solvable objection into a silent loss.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','Which preparation most improves a seller position before a negotiation?','Knowing the value at stake for the buyer, the seller walk-away position, the tradeable variables and the likely alternatives on both sides','Rehearsing responses to price objections','Securing manager approval for a discount in advance','Deciding the lowest acceptable price','A','Negotiating strength comes from knowing value, alternatives and what can be traded, which is what allows movement without loss. Pre-approving a discount tends to guarantee that it is used.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','hard','A buyer asks the seller to backdate a contract into the previous quarter so the buyer can use lapsed budget. What should the seller do?','Backdate only the signature page','Ask finance to issue the invoice with the earlier date','Backdate the contract since the commercial substance is unchanged','Refuse, explain that dating a contract to a period in which it was not agreed misstates both parties records, and offer legitimate alternatives such as phased start or payment terms','D','Backdating misstates the records of both parties and can constitute falsification of accounts regardless of intent. Legitimate structuring of start dates and payment terms usually solves the underlying budget problem.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Objection Handling and Negotiation','exam','A stalled deal has gone quiet after a strong proposal. What is the most effective action?','Send repeated follow-up emails asking for an update','Return to the sponsor with new information relevant to the business case and a specific question about the decision process and any changed priority','Discount to restart the conversation','Escalate to the buyer senior management','B','Silence usually means priorities changed or an internal obstacle appeared, so the seller re-engages with new value and a direct question about the process. Repeated status requests give the buyer nothing to respond to.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','What is the most reliable indicator that a deal is ready to close?','The buyer has confirmed the business case, the decision process is complete and the remaining steps and dates are agreed','The seller has sent a proposal','The quarter is ending','The buyer has attended a demonstration','A','Readiness is evidenced by an agreed business case and a completed decision path, not by seller activity or calendar pressure. Closing attempts before those exist create the classic late-quarter slip.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','Which closing behaviour is consistent with professional selling standards?','Contacting the buyer superior to apply pressure','Manufacturing artificial deadlines and expiring discounts','Asking directly for the decision once the value and process are established, and agreeing the next step whatever the answer','Repeatedly asking for the order until the buyer agrees','C','A direct, timely request for the decision respects the buyer and resolves the opportunity either way. Artificial urgency and pressure tactics damage the relationship and are excluded by professional codes of conduct.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','hard','After the contract is signed the seller learns that delivery will slip by four weeks. What should the seller do?','Wait until the customer notices','Inform the customer immediately with the revised plan, the cause and the mitigation, and confirm what the customer needs to protect its own commitments','Inform the customer at the next scheduled review','Offer a discount and say nothing about the cause','B','Early disclosure with a plan preserves the trust the account depends on and lets the customer protect its own dependencies. Concealment converts a schedule problem into a relationship failure.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','What should a seller do in the first thirty days after a new customer signs?','Ask for a reference immediately','Present the upsell catalogue','Move immediately to the next prospect','Ensure the handover to delivery is complete, confirm the success criteria agreed in the sale and stay engaged until first value is achieved','D','The commitments made during the sale become the delivery criteria, and the seller is the only person who heard them all. First value is also the precondition for renewal, reference and expansion.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','Which approach to account expansion is most likely to succeed?','Discounting the additional modules heavily','Contacting new stakeholders without informing the current sponsor','Offering the full product catalogue at renewal','Identifying an adjacent problem, evidencing the value already delivered and proposing the expansion against that problem','D','Expansion is a new sale that must stand on its own problem and on the credibility earned by delivered value. Catalogue offers and discounts without a problem produce no internal case for the buyer.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','A customer complains about a service failure. What is the professional standard for the seller response?','Refer the customer to the support process and step back','Own the issue with the customer, coordinate the internal response, keep the customer informed on a committed cadence and confirm the resolution','Apologise and offer a discount immediately','Escalate to the customer executive sponsor','B','The relationship holder owns the customer experience of the failure, which means coordinating internally and communicating on a committed cadence. Handing the customer to a queue at the moment of failure is where accounts are lost.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','hard','A seller commission plan pays on booked revenue, and a large deal can be booked now but is likely to churn within two quarters. What does professional practice require?','Reduce the contract value to lower the risk','Book the deal, since the plan rewards it','Raise the churn risk with management, address the underlying fit problem with the customer and not book a deal the seller believes will fail','Book the deal and warn the delivery team informally','C','Selling a customer something the seller expects to fail breaches the duty of honest dealing and damages the company and the customer. A compensation plan that rewards the behaviour does not authorise it.'),
('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)','Closing and Account Management','exam','What is the correct treatment of customer information a seller obtained during a sales cycle after moving to a new employer?','Confidential customer information belongs to the former employer and the customer and must not be used or disclosed','It may be used if it was never written down','It may be shared with the new employer marketing team','It may be used freely because it is in the seller memory','A','Confidentiality obligations attach to the information, not to the medium in which it was held or the seller current employment. Using it at a new employer exposes the seller and the new employer to claims.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CSP (Certified Sales Professional)');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','exam','An organisation captures supplier trade licences as photographs taken on mobile phones. Which capture improvement most increases downstream usability?','Storing the images in a shared folder by month','Renaming the files with the supplier name','Increasing the image resolution only','Capturing to a standard format with image quality control, applying text recognition and extracting the licence number, issuer, activity and expiry as indexed metadata','D','Usability downstream depends on machine-readable content and indexed metadata, not on the image alone. Extracting the identifying fields at capture is what allows later search, expiry monitoring and verification against the register.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','exam','What is the principal advantage of capturing information at the point of creation rather than later?','Metadata and context are available at the point of creation, so classification, retention and access decisions are accurate and cheaper than reconstructing them later','It guarantees the information is accurate','It removes the need for retention rules','It reduces the cost of scanning hardware','A','Context decays quickly, and capturing at creation records the metadata that later classification would have to reconstruct by inference. Later capture is both more expensive and less reliable.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','hard','A document capture pipeline reports ninety-eight percent text recognition accuracy, yet supplier name search fails frequently. What is the most likely cause?','Users are searching incorrectly','The scanner resolution is too low','Character accuracy is measured across all characters while the errors concentrate in proper nouns and identifiers, so field-level accuracy on the indexed fields is what matters','The search index is too large','C','Aggregate character accuracy hides poor performance on the small number of characters that carry the identity of a record. Capture quality is therefore measured at field level on the fields used to retrieve.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','exam','Which capture control best supports later evidential use of a scanned original?','Storing the scan in a compressed format to save space','Recording capture metadata including operator, date, device and a checksum, and documenting the process under which the original was scanned','Discarding the original immediately after scanning','Applying a watermark to the image','B','Evidential weight depends on being able to show what was captured, by whom and that it has not changed since. A documented process and integrity value are what allow the image to stand in place of the original.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','exam','A business unit creates records in a messaging application that is not managed by the organisation. What is the correct information management response?','Ban all messaging use and consider the matter closed','Recognise that records are being created outside the managed environment, decide which content is a record, and either bring the channel under management or provide a compliant alternative','Export the messages annually to a shared drive','Treat messaging content as ephemeral by definition','B','Records are defined by content and function, not by the system they were made in, so unmanaged channels create unmanaged records. The response is to govern or replace the channel, since prohibition alone does not stop the behaviour.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','exam','What distinguishes a record from other information in an organisation?','Its author seniority','Its file format','Evidence of a business activity or transaction that the organisation must be able to rely on, and which is therefore subject to retention and integrity requirements','The fact that it is stored in a repository','C','A record evidences an activity and must remain reliable and retrievable for as long as required. Format, location and authorship do not determine record status.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Creating and Capturing Information','hard','An organisation wants to reduce manual keying from supplier invoices arriving in many layouts. Which approach is most robust?','Extraction that identifies fields by context and validates the result against master data and business rules, with confidence-based exception routing','Requiring all suppliers to use one template','Manual keying with double entry','A fixed template for each supplier layout','A','Layout-specific templates break whenever a supplier changes its form, while contextual extraction with validation degrades gracefully. Confidence-based routing keeps human attention on the cases that need it.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','exam','What is the primary purpose of a file plan or classification scheme?','To reduce the number of folders','To standardise file names','To decide which team owns each shared drive','To provide a consistent structure that links records to business activities and their retention and access rules','D','A classification scheme connects records to the activities they evidence, which is what allows retention and access to be applied consistently. Naming and folder counts are consequences, not the purpose.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','exam','A taxonomy has grown to four hundred terms and users cannot find documents. What is the most likely problem?','The search engine is underpowered','The taxonomy reflects internal structure rather than how users seek information, and the term set exceeds what users can navigate','There are too few metadata fields','Documents are not being tagged at all','B','Taxonomies fail when they encode the organisation chart rather than user information-seeking behaviour, and depth beyond a usable size guarantees inconsistent tagging. Search cannot compensate for a structure users cannot apply.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','hard','Which metadata design decision most improves retrieval of supplier verification records?','Recording the file size','Adding a free-text notes field','Using controlled vocabulary for supplier status and document type with validated identifiers such as licence number and entity identifier','Recording the uploading user name','C','Controlled values and validated identifiers make retrieval and matching deterministic across systems. Free text and system-generated attributes add no retrieval power for the questions the business asks.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','exam','Why is auto-classification usually deployed with a confidence threshold and human review queue?','Because classification accuracy varies by content type and the consequences of misclassification for retention and access require an exception path','Because machine classification is always less accurate than manual','To slow the volume of ingestion','To satisfy the vendor licensing model','A','Automatic classification is reliable for the bulk and unreliable at the margins, and misclassification carries retention and access consequences. A threshold with review targets human effort where the risk is.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','exam','What is the difference between a taxonomy and an ontology in information management?','An ontology is a taxonomy with more terms','A taxonomy applies only to documents','They are alternative names for the same structure','A taxonomy arranges terms hierarchically, while an ontology also defines typed relationships between entities and supports inference','D','A taxonomy provides hierarchy, while an ontology adds typed relationships from which additional facts can be derived. The distinction matters when the requirement is to connect entities rather than to file documents.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','exam','Users consistently tag documents with the wrong department value. Which intervention is most likely to work?','Adding a mandatory field warning','Auditing tags monthly and correcting them','Publishing the tagging policy again','Defaulting the value from the business context of the capture process so the user does not have to choose','D','Removing a manual decision by deriving it from context is more reliable than asking users to comply correctly. Policy restatement and downstream correction both leave the error source in place.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Organizing and Categorizing Information','hard','An organisation merges two repositories with incompatible classification schemes. What is the sound approach?','Map both schemes to a target scheme with a documented crosswalk, migrate metadata with validation and retain the source values for traceability','Discard the metadata from the smaller repository','Reclassify everything manually','Keep both schemes and let users choose','A','A documented crosswalk with retained source values allows migration to be validated and errors traced afterwards. Discarding metadata or leaving parallel schemes destroys retrieval and retention consistency.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','exam','What is the essential relationship between information governance and records management?','Information governance applies only to unstructured content','They are the same discipline','Information governance sets the accountability, policy and decision framework for all information assets, within which records management applies its specific controls','Records management governs information governance','C','Governance establishes accountability and policy across information assets, while records management applies retention, integrity and disposition to records specifically. Treating them as identical usually leaves non-record information ungoverned.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','exam','An organisation holds personal data of UAE-resident suppliers and their staff. Which practice aligns with the UAE personal data protection law principles?','Retain all data indefinitely for audit purposes','Process for specified lawful purposes, retain only as long as necessary, keep it accurate and secure, and honour data subject rights','Publish the data internally so any employee can verify suppliers','Transfer the data abroad without any assessment','B','The UAE framework applies purpose limitation, storage limitation, accuracy, security and data subject rights, and it conditions cross-border transfer. Indefinite retention and unrestricted internal publication breach the limitation and security principles.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','hard','A litigation hold is issued over a matter. What is the immediate effect on scheduled destruction?','Destruction continues because the retention schedule takes precedence','Scheduled destruction of potentially relevant information is suspended and the suspension is documented and communicated to custodians and system owners','Only paper records are held','The hold applies after the case is filed in court','B','A legal hold overrides the retention schedule for potentially relevant information from the point that litigation is reasonably anticipated. Documenting and communicating the suspension is what makes the hold defensible and effective.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','exam','Who should own an information governance policy for it to be effective?','The records management team alone','The external auditor','The information technology department','An accountable executive with a cross-functional steering group including legal, risk, records, security and the business','D','Information governance requires decisions across legal, risk, security and business lines, so it needs executive accountability and cross-functional authority. Ownership inside a single function produces policy nobody outside it follows.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','exam','What is the most useful measure of an information governance programme effectiveness?','Evidence of outcomes such as reduced redundant and obsolete data, retention applied at scale, defensible disposition and faster discovery response','The size of the governance team','The number of training sessions delivered','The number of policies published','A','Governance is judged on measurable outcomes in the information estate and in the organisation ability to respond. Policy and activity counts describe effort rather than effect.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','exam','A department wants to keep everything forever because storage is inexpensive. What is the strongest argument against?','Users prefer smaller repositories','Storage costs will eventually rise','Retaining beyond the required period increases discovery cost, breach exposure and data protection non-compliance, and dilutes the value of what must be kept','Backups become slower','C','Over-retention converts inexpensive storage into expensive risk through discovery scope, breach exposure and data protection obligations. Cost of storage is the smallest element of the total cost of keeping information.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Governing Information','hard','An organisation must apply retention across a mixture of managed repositories, cloud collaboration and legacy file shares. What is the most defensible approach?','Rely on user-driven deletion','Apply retention only where it is technically easy','Define retention by business rule, apply it through native controls in each platform where possible, remediate or migrate the rest, and document exceptions with a plan','Migrate everything into one repository first','C','Defensibility comes from a consistent rule applied through the best available control in each platform, with exceptions documented and scheduled for remediation. Waiting on a single-repository migration leaves the estate ungoverned for years.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','exam','Which characteristic makes a process a good candidate for information capture and workflow automation?','It is high volume, rule-based, dependent on information from documents and has a measurable error or cycle-time cost','It has never been documented','It involves few exceptions but no measurable cost','It is performed by senior staff','A','Automation returns value where volume, rules and document dependence meet a measurable cost. Without a measured baseline the benefit cannot be shown and the project cannot be prioritised.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','exam','What is the risk of automating a process without redesigning it first?','The vendor will not support it','Users will not need training','The automation will run too quickly for users','Existing inefficiencies and unnecessary steps are encoded and made harder to change','D','Automating an unexamined process fixes its current shape in software and raises the cost of later change. Analysing and simplifying before automating is what produces the benefit.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','hard','A supplier onboarding workflow automatically approves records where extracted licence data matches the register. Which control is essential?','A weekly report of approvals','Straight-through processing limited by confidence thresholds and validation rules, with exception routing, full audit logging and periodic sampling of automated decisions','A manager sign-off on every record','Disabling automation for large suppliers','B','Automated decisions need thresholds, exceptions, an audit trail and sampling to show they remain correct over time. Requiring human sign-off on every record removes the benefit while adding a rubber stamp.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','exam','What role does a business rules engine play in an information-intensive process?','It externalises decision logic so rules can be changed and audited without changing the application code','It replaces the workflow engine','It performs text recognition','It stores the documents','A','Separating rules from code allows the business to change and audit decisions at the speed the business changes. Coupling rules into application logic makes every policy change a development project.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','exam','How should exceptions be handled in an automated document process?','Processed manually with no record','Returned to the sender without explanation','Routed to a defined queue with the reason, the extracted values and the source document, and measured so recurring causes are fixed','Held until a monthly review','C','Exceptions must be resolvable quickly and must feed back into the process, which requires context and measurement. Unmeasured exception queues grow until the automation benefit disappears.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','exam','Which metric best demonstrates the value of automating an invoice process?','The number of documents stored','Cost per invoice, straight-through processing rate, cycle time and exception rate measured against the pre-automation baseline','The number of workflows configured','The number of users trained','B','Value is shown by unit cost, throughput and quality measures against a baseline captured before the change. Configuration and training counts describe the project, not the outcome.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Automating Information-Intensive Processes','hard','An automated classification model degrades over six months as document mixes change. What practice addresses this?','Replacing the model with rules','Increasing the confidence threshold permanently','Retraining only when users complain','Monitoring accuracy against sampled ground truth, alerting on drift and retraining on a defined cadence with version control of the model and its training data','D','Model performance drifts with the content it sees, so it is monitored against sampled truth and retrained on a controlled cadence with versioning. Raising the threshold only moves more work to the exception queue without restoring accuracy.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','exam','A retention schedule specifies seven years for supplier contracts. When does the retention period normally begin?','On the date the file was created in the repository','On the date of the last access','On the date the document was scanned','At the defined trigger event, commonly the expiry or termination of the contract','D','Retention runs from a defined event that makes the record inactive, which for contracts is normally expiry or termination. Anchoring on capture or access dates destroys and retains records at the wrong times.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','exam','What makes a disposition defensible?','That it was performed quickly','That it followed an approved schedule, was applied consistently, respected legal holds and was documented with evidence of what was destroyed and when','That the records were of low value','That a manager approved it verbally','B','Defensibility rests on a documented, consistently applied schedule with holds respected and destruction evidenced. Individual judgements without records cannot be defended afterwards.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','hard','Records must remain readable for twenty-five years. Which strategy best addresses digital preservation risk?','Keep the original application installed indefinitely','Store on the current storage platform and rely on backups','Use open, well-documented formats, plan format migration, maintain integrity checks and preserve descriptive and technical metadata alongside the content','Print everything to paper','C','Long-term readability is threatened by format and software obsolescence rather than by media failure alone, so open formats, planned migration and preserved metadata are the controls. Backups protect availability, not intelligibility.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','exam','What should happen to redundant, obsolete and trivial content identified in a file share assessment?','It should be assessed against the retention schedule and holds, then defensibly disposed of, with genuine records migrated under management','It should be deleted immediately','It should be left in place and excluded from search','It should be archived to cheaper storage','A','Moving unmanaged content to cheaper storage keeps every risk and adds none of the benefit. Assessment against schedule and holds allows disposal of the trivial and management of the real records.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','exam','A data subject requests erasure of personal data held in supplier onboarding files. What determines the response?','The request applies only to marketing data','The request must always be fulfilled in full','Whether a legal basis or obligation requires continued retention, in which case the specific data required is retained and the remainder is erased, with the decision recorded','The request may be refused if the data is in a backup','C','Erasure rights are qualified by legal retention obligations and other lawful bases, so the assessment is data by data rather than all or nothing. Recording the reasoning is what evidences the decision later.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Managing the Information Lifecycle','exam','Which practice most reduces the cost of responding to discovery or a regulatory request?','Retaining everything so nothing is missing','Applying retention and disposition consistently so the estate contains what the organisation must keep, indexed and retrievable','Storing all content in one uncategorised repository','Encrypting all archives','B','Response cost scales with the volume that must be searched and reviewed, so a governed, indexed estate is dramatically cheaper to search. Keeping everything maximises exactly the volume that drives the cost.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','exam','What should be established before selecting an information management platform?','The storage volume discount','The user interface preferences','The preferred vendor shortlist','The business requirements, information inventory, governance rules and success measures the platform must serve','D','Requirements, the information inventory and the governance rules determine what the platform must do and how success will be judged. Selecting first makes those decisions implicitly and usually badly.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','exam','Which factor most commonly causes an information management implementation to fail?','Neglect of change management, so users continue working in the old locations and the new system holds an incomplete record','An unsuitable database engine','Too many metadata fields','Insufficient storage capacity','A','Adoption determines whether the record is complete, and a system users bypass is worse than none because it creates false confidence. Technical factors are usually solvable where adoption is not.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','hard','A migration must move two million documents with metadata into a new repository. Which approach best protects integrity?','Migrate in validated batches with reconciliation of counts, checksums and metadata mapping, a documented rollback and a period of parallel access','Ask users to move their own documents','Migrate only the documents accessed in the last year','Migrate all content in one cutover to minimise disruption','A','Batch migration with reconciliation of counts, integrity values and metadata is what proves nothing was lost or altered, and rollback covers the failures that do occur. A single cutover offers no point at which errors can be detected cheaply.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','exam','How should a business case for an information management programme be constructed?','On the number of documents to be managed','On the vendor published return on investment figures','On the cost of the software licences alone','On measurable outcomes such as reduced discovery and storage cost, reduced risk exposure, cycle-time improvement and productivity, against the total cost of ownership','D','A defensible case measures outcomes the organisation can verify against the full cost of ownership. Vendor figures and volume counts do not survive finance review.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','exam','What is the purpose of an information audit or inventory at the start of a programme?','To identify which employees create the most documents','To establish what information exists, where it is held, who owns it, its value and risk, and which obligations attach to it','To calculate the storage budget','To select the taxonomy software','B','You cannot govern what has not been located and characterised, so the inventory establishes holdings, ownership, value, risk and obligations. Every later decision on retention, access and migration depends on it.'),
('CIP (Certified Information Professional)','Implementing an Information Management Solution','exam','Which governance artefact should be in place before a new repository is opened to users?','A backup schedule only','A vendor support contract','Agreed classification, metadata, retention and access rules configured in the platform, with defined ownership','A user satisfaction survey','C','Opening a repository without configured classification, retention and access rules creates another ungoverned store that must later be remediated. Ownership and rules must exist before content arrives, not after.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CIP (Certified Information Professional)');

do $proof$
declare
  r record;
begin
  for r in
    select c.name, (select count(*) from public.certification_exam_question q where q.certification = c.name) as n
    from (values
      ('CFE (Certified Fraud Examiner)'),
      ('CSAP (Certified Strategic Alliance Professional)'),
      ('CSP (Certified Sales Professional)'),
      ('CIP (Certified Information Professional)')
    ) as c(name)
  loop
    if r.n < 40 then
      raise exception 'certification bank % has only % questions on production, expected at least 40', r.name, r.n;
    end if;
  end loop;
end
$proof$;
